    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> This Beautiful Day</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> It's Raining! I Need an Umbrella.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td colspan="3" style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td colspan="3" style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td colspan="3" style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Illustrate the main ideas from a reading in a creative project. Reading: Summarize a text using a graphic organizer. Phonemic Awareness: Blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable or two-syllable words if these words have been previously encountered.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td colspan="3" style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can illustrate main ideas from reading about weather by drawing pictures (e.g., a sun for "It's sunny."). Can summarize weather texts with a graphic organizer (e.g., "It's sunny" under "weather category"). Can blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable and two-syllable weather-related words (e.g., blending /ˈr-eɪn-i/ in "rainy" and /s-n-oʊ/ in "snow").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0e0e0;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and shows large flashcards of different weather types (sun, rain, cloud, wind) and related items (umbrella, coat, boots). The teacher asks, "How do you feel today?" and models responses like "I feel happy!" or "I feel sad." The teacher then points to the "rain" flashcard and says, "It's raining! What do I need?" (umbrella, coat, boots). Students will point or try to say the words they learned in Lesson 1.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of a rainy day and an umbrella. The teacher says slowly and clearly, "It's raining. I need an umbrella. It's cold." The teacher then writes and reads aloud a simple sentence on the board: "It's a rainy day." The teacher points to the words as they are read, emphasizing /r/ in "rainy". The teacher models blending phonemes for "rain": /r/-/eɪn/ → "rain".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses visuals and simple questions to check understanding.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a sunny picture) "Is it rainy?" (No!)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding an umbrella) "Do I need this for the sun or the rain?" (Rain!)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated narrative text titled "Milo's Rainy Day" presented on large flashcards or a projector. The text will consist of 3-4 simple sentences per slide/page, using present simple, contractions ("it's"), and target vocabulary (rain, umbrella, coat, boots, happy, sad, play, sky, rainbow).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The story might read:</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It's raining! The sky is grey."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Milo is sad. He can't play outside."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Milo needs his umbrella and boots."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Look! A bright rainbow! Milo feels happy now."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student reads and understands the main idea of short, simple, illustrated texts about familiar themes. (Reading comprehension and extracting main ideas).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen as the teacher reads the story aloud, pointing to words. Then, the teacher reads it again, and students follow along silently.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is Milo's story about? Is it about a sunny day or a rainy day?" (Students point to "rainy" flashcard or say "rainy").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher states sentences, and students show thumbs up for True, thumbs down for False.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Milo is happy when it rains." (False)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Milo needs an umbrella." (True)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> The teacher shows pictures of "Milo sad" and "Milo happy." Students read the sentence "Milo is sad" and point to the matching picture. Then "Milo feels happy now" and point to that picture.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension of simple sentences, identifying key vocabulary, and understanding context. Focus on grammar: "It's," "I feel," "he needs." Vocabulary: rain, sunny, umbrella, happy, sad, play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Cloze Reading):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with simple sentences from "Milo's Rainy Day" that have one word missing. Students read the sentence and choose the correct word from a provided word bank (e.g., "It's ______. The sky is grey." Options: sunny, raining, windy. Students circle "raining"). This activity reinforces reading for specific information and vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Substitution Drill (Sentence Reading):</b> The teacher displays a sentence on the board or flashcard: "It's a sunny day. I feel happy." The teacher then changes the adjective "sunny" to "rainy," and "happy" to "sad." Students read the new sentence chorally: "It's a rainy day. I feel sad." The teacher guides them to blend /s/ in "sunny" and /r/ in "rainy" while reading. This helps them read and understand sentence structures with new vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Read and Match):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of short, simple sentences (e.g., "It's raining. I need an umbrella." "It's sunny. I can play.") and corresponding picture cards. Students take turns *reading* a sentence aloud to their partner, who then *matches* it to the correct picture. They check each other's reading and matching.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Weather Story Illustration and Summary"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are given a blank sheet of paper divided into 3-4 sections. The teacher provides a simplified written version of "Milo's Rainy Day" story (or a similar short text about weather and feelings) for each student. Students will first *re-read* the story independently or in small groups.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">For each section of their paper, they will:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. *Illustrate* a main idea from a specific part of the story (e.g., section 1: drawing rain falling and a sad Milo; section 2: drawing Milo with an umbrella; section 3: drawing a rainbow and a happy Milo).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. *Write* one simple sentence below each drawing to summarize that main idea, using the target vocabulary and grammar (e.g., "It's rainy." "Milo needs an umbrella." "Milo feels happy."). This activity directly addresses the specific objective of illustrating main ideas from a reading and summarizing text using a graphic organizer (their illustrated paper).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative Assessment)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete their "My Weather Story Illustration and Summary" project. They then exchange their completed work with a partner for a quick peer check, *reading* each other's sentences and confirming the illustrations match. Finally, students present their own illustrated story to the teacher, *reading* their summary sentences aloud.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student illustrates main ideas from the reading.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student writes simple, coherent sentences to summarize.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student uses target vocabulary (rainy, umbrella, happy, sad) accurately.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student demonstrates effort in blending phonemes when reading words (e.g., /r-eɪn/ for "rain").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student's pronunciation is mostly intelligible when reading.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a simple exit ticket by drawing a circle around one of these faces: 🙂 (I understood the story very well), 😐 (I understood some of the story), 🙁 (I need help to understand the story). They also circle three words they read today (e.g., rain, sun, happy, umbrella) from a short list provided.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite picture to draw from the story today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which weather word was easy for you to read?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? The illustrated story seemed engaging and the combination of drawing and writing helped reinforce comprehension. Students were able to identify main ideas from the text.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking)? Some students found blending two-syllable words challenging, though most managed one-syllable words. I will need to provide more explicit pronunciation practice for these two-syllable words during speaking activities in Lesson 3. The vocabulary and basic sentence structures for describing weather and feelings are established, which will be a good base for oral interaction.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 3, students will use their "My Weather Story Illustration" projects as visual prompts to speak about weather and feelings, using the learned vocabulary and grammar in pair work and class discussions.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min (optional or integrated)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>